Let us analyze an example of a reaction involving solids or liquids.
When calcium carbonate monohydrate (\ce{CaCO3 * H2O}) is heated, it undergoes a two-step thermal decomposition process that ultimately produces carbon dioxide gas, as shown below:
\begin{center}\ce{CaCO3 * H2O_{(s)} <=> CaO_{(s)} + CO2_{(g)} + H2O_{(l)}}\end{center}
Experiments have found that pure solids and liquids do not affect chemical equilibrium or appear in equilibrium constant expressions because their concentrations remain constant throughout a reaction. For the example above:
\begin{equation*}
  K_{c}=\big[ \ce{CO2} \big]
\end{equation*}
Differently, for a reaction involving aqueous solutions, for example:
\\\ce{HCl_{(Aq)} <=> H^+_{(Aq)} + Cl^-_{(Aq)}}\hfill $K_{c}=\frac{[\ce{H^+}]\cdot [\ce{Cl^-}]}{[\ce{HCl}]}$\\
Overall, the expression of $K_c$ can only include gases (g) or aqueous solutions (aq), leaving solids and liquids out.